<h1>Orcraft Statuses</h1>
<p>Every component result carries a status saying how it went and descriptions saying why. This page
  covers the statuses themselves and the mechanisms that set them - <code>checkpoint</code>,
  <code>handle_errors</code> and the description fields - before
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/">Orcraft Result Fields</a> covers what else a
  result can carry.</p>

<h2 id="statuses">Statuses</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Status</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-pass">PASS</code></td>
      <td>The component ran and every expected value matched the actual one.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-fail">FAIL</code></td>
      <td>The component ran and found a mismatch: an expected value did not match the actual one. This is the component doing its job - a real finding about the system under test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-error">ERROR</code></td>
      <td>An exception - an unexpected failure the component was not testing for. It could not reach a verdict, so there is no finding.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The distinction between <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> and <code class="status-error">ERROR</code> is the one that matters in practice.
  <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> is an <em>expected</em> kind of failure: the component compared expected against actual,
  they differed, and it reported that - the system under test is wrong. <code class="status-error">ERROR</code> is an
  <em>unexpected</em> failure: something raised - a missing file, a dead connection, a bug in the test
  itself - before a comparison could be made, so nothing has been established about the system under
  test either way. A suite where half the results are errors has an infrastructure problem, not a
  quality problem, and Orcraft counts the two separately so that difference is visible at a glance.</p>

<p>A result starts at <code class="status-pass">PASS</code> and is moved from there - by a failing checkpoint, by an
  assignment to one of the descriptions, or by an uncaught exception, which is what produces
  <code class="status-error">ERROR</code>.</p>

<p><code>OrcraftStatus</code> also declares an <code>ABORT</code> member. Nothing in the framework or the
  controller produces it, so a result never carries it; a run halted from the controller is shown there
  as stopped rather than reported through a component status.</p>

<h3 id="setting-a-status">Setting a status</h3>
<p>A test rarely assigns <code>status</code> directly. Most of the time it states a finding and lets the
  framework derive the status from it:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import OrcraftStatus

...

def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> OrcraftTestResult:
	case.result = MyTestResult()

	response = self.query_system(self.config.payload_size)

	# ERROR: the case cannot produce a finding at all, so say why and stop.
	if response is None:
		case.result.status = OrcraftStatus.ERROR
		case.result.error_description = "The system under test returned no response."
		return case.result

	case.result.measured_value = response.value

	# FAIL: a failing checkpoint records the finding and carries on.
	self.checkpoint(
		response.value <= self.config.threshold,
		f"Measured {response.value}, within the threshold.",
		f"Measured {response.value}, above the threshold of {self.config.threshold}.",
	)

	# FAIL: assigning a failure description has the same effect as a failed checkpoint.
	if response.code != 0:
		case.result.failure_description = f"The system reported code {response.code}."

	# Still PASS, but worth flagging.
	if response.retries:
		case.result.warning_description = f"Succeeded after {response.retries} retries."

	return case.result') }}

<p>Each status has its own route:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Status</th>
      <th>How a test produces it</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-pass">PASS</code></td>
      <td>The value a result starts at. Nothing to set.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-fail">FAIL</code></td>
      <td>A failing <a class="text-accent" href="#checkpoints"><code>checkpoint</code></a>, or assigning <code class="status-fail">failure_description</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-error">ERROR</code></td>
      <td>An uncaught exception, a checkpoint with <code>is_error=True</code>, or assigning <code>status</code> together with <code class="status-error">error_description</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Note what the example does <em>not</em> do: it never sets <code>OrcraftStatus.FAIL</code> by hand. Going
  through a checkpoint or a description means the reason travels with the status, so the log and the
  controller show why the case failed and not merely that it did. Assigning
  <code class="status-fail">failure_description</code> on a passing result moves it to <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> on its own, which is
  why the two are never set together.</p>

<p>Only <code class="status-error">ERROR</code> is worth assigning explicitly, and only when the case has to stop early - it is
  the one status that says the finding is missing rather than negative.</p>

<h3 id="reading-a-status">Reading a status</h3>
<p>Statuses compare as an enum, which is occasionally useful when a caller wants to branch on the outcome
  of a nested component rather than just propagate it:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('flow_result = flow.run()

if flow_result.status is OrcraftStatus.ERROR:
	Logger.log("Skipping verification - the flow could not complete.", color=Logger.Color.YELLOW)
	return case.result') }}

<p>For the usual case - copy the outcome upward and stop if it failed - reach for
  <a class="text-accent" href="#handle-errors"><code>handle_errors</code></a> instead of comparing
  statuses by hand.</p>

<h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
<p><code>checkpoint</code> is the framework's assertion, available on every component. It takes a
  condition and the message to log for each outcome:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('self.checkpoint(
	measured <= threshold,
	f"Measured {measured}, within the threshold of {threshold}.",
	f"Measured {measured}, above the threshold of {threshold}.",
	details="Measured over the full run, not a single sample.",
)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Argument</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>result</code></td>
      <td>The condition being asserted.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>pass_msg</code></td>
      <td>Logged when the condition holds.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>fail_msg</code></td>
      <td>Logged when it does not, and used as the failure description.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>details</code></td>
      <td>Optional extra context, logged alongside the outcome.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>is_error</code></td>
      <td>Treat a failed check as <code class="status-error">ERROR</code> rather than <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code>, abandoning the surrounding scope.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Both outcomes are logged, so the log records what was checked and not only what broke. This is what
  makes a passing Orcraft run readable as evidence rather than as silence.</p>

<p>A failing checkpoint sets the component to <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> with the failure message as its
  description, and then <em>continues</em>. That is deliberate: one case can report every problem it
  found instead of only the first. Pass <code>is_error=True</code> when a failed check means the rest of
  the work would be meaningless - a missing file, an application that did not start - and the component
  is marked <code class="status-error">ERROR</code> and the scope abandoned instead.</p>

<p>Inside a <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/lifecycle-hooks/">setup or teardown hook</a> a failing
  checkpoint is always treated as an error, whether or not <code>is_error</code> was passed. A lifecycle
  assertion that does not hold means the work it was guarding should not proceed.</p>

<h2 id="warnings">Warnings</h2>
<p>A warning records something worth surfacing that does not make the result wrong - a retry that
  succeeded, output that was unusual but valid, a slower run than expected. It is raised by assigning
  <code class="status-warning">warning_description</code> on the result:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('case.result.warning_description = f"Retried {retries} times before succeeding."') }}

<p>The status is unaffected - setting <code class="status-warning">warning_description</code> never
  moves a result off <code class="status-pass">PASS</code> - but the case is counted as a case with
  warnings, and that count aggregates up through the group and suite results. A run can therefore be
  entirely green and still tell you where to look.</p>

<h2 id="handle-errors">Propagating statuses</h2>
<p>Components do not raise their way up the hierarchy - they return results, and the caller decides.
  <code>handle_errors</code> copies status and descriptions from one result to another and returns
  <code>True</code> when the caller should stop:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('flow_result = flow.run()

if self.handle_errors(flow_result, case.result):
	return case.result') }}

<p>This is the idiom to use after every flow call in a case and after every step call in a
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/">flow</a>. It keeps the failure attached to the component
  that produced it while still surfacing it at every level above, so the controller can show which step
  of which flow of which case of which test failed - rather than a single stack trace at the top.</p>

<h3 id="handle-errors-signature">Signature</h3>
{{ codeblock.block('def handle_errors(self, source_result, target_result) -> bool') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Argument</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>source_result</code></td>
      <td>The result just produced by the nested component - a flow result in a case, a step result in a flow.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>target_result</code></td>
      <td>The result of the caller, which receives the outcome - <code>case.result</code> in a case, <code>self.result</code> in a flow.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Returns</td>
      <td><code>True</code> when the source status is <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> or <code class="status-error">ERROR</code>, meaning the caller should stop; <code>False</code> on <code class="status-pass">PASS</code>, including a pass with a warning.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>It copies four fields from source to target: <code>status</code>, <code class="status-fail">failure_description</code>,
  <code class="status-error">error_description</code> and <code class="status-warning">warning_description</code>. The copy is unconditional - the last
  call wins. That is harmless for failures, because the idiom returns on the first one, but it means a
  warning carried by an earlier flow is overwritten when a later flow passes cleanly. Read
  <code class="status-warning">warning_description</code> off the earlier result before the next call if you need to keep it.</p>

<p><code>handle_errors</code> is available on <code>OrcraftTest</code> and <code>OrcraftTestFlow</code>
  - the two components that call something beneath them. A step runs nothing below it, and groups and
  suites aggregate their children's results on their own, so none of them carries the method.</p>
